## 1. Evidence protocol (charter §1)
- Tag every claim about repo state: `VERIFIED(<path/cmd>)` (you read/ran it
  this session), `INFERRED(<basis>)`, or `UNVERIFIED`. Hedging ≠ a tag.
- Before calling anything *missing*, run the check (`ls`/`find`/`grep`) and
  cite it. "Absent" needs positive evidence of absence.
- Counts (tests, endpoints) come from a command run this session, not memory.
- Can't verify? Say so and list it under **Unknowns** — never fill silently.

## 2. Diagram & architecture claims (charter §2)
- Every diagram caption is labelled **OBSERVED** (every arrow backed by a
  verified import/call/config) or **PROPOSED / INFERRED**.
- No dependency arrow without grepping the actual import/call. No grep, no
  arrow — or it goes in a PROPOSED diagram. Diagrams follow C4 (C1–C2 always;
  C3–C4 only for components under active change).

## 3. Component & scope gates (charter §4)
- No new top-level component/package/service without an accepted ADR stating
  purpose, why existing code can't absorb it, and expected lifespan.
- **Duplication scan first:** grep domain nouns across components before
  adding functionality. Overlap → P0 finding, stop for a decision. Two
  components solving one problem is the costliest multi-session failure.
- `*-mvp`/`*-spike`/`*-poc` = disposable; needs a promote-or-delete expiry
  note. Do not extend a spike as if permanent — ask: promote or delete?
- Match doc fidelity to horizon: H1 (sprint) full spec/tests/ADRs; H2
  (quarter) RFC only; H3 (exploratory) spike notes only. Both directions
  are violations.

## 4. Review & analysis output contract (charter §6)
When asked to review/audit/analyze, output in this order, on ~one screen:
1. **TL;DR** — max 3 lines, most consequential finding first.
2. **P0 findings** — 2–4 items; each = one-line claim + evidence tag +
   action. (P0 = architecture risk / duplication / doc–code divergence.)
3. **Component matrix** — ONE table for all components.
4. **Unknowns** — everything you couldn't verify this session.
5. **Next question** — exactly one, whose answer unblocks the most.
- Praise names a trade-off ("X buys Y at cost Z") or is omitted.
  Unfalsifiable compliments are forbidden.

## 5. Three-question gate for any new document (Appendix A.9)
Before creating any doc not mandated by this profile, answer in the commit/PR:
1. **Who reads this?** (a named role — "nobody" kills it)
2. **What decision/action does it enable?** ("none" kills it)
3. **What event triggers its update?** ("nothing" kills it — it will rot)
Any blank answer → don't write the doc. The pre-commit hook enforces the
`> Reader: … | Enables: … | Update-trigger: …` header on new `.md` files.

## 6. This project's profile — Type {{ project_type }} (Appendix A)
{% if project_type == 'A' -%}
**Type A — Library / SDK.**
- MUST: README (install + 3 usage examples), API reference from
  docstrings/types, CHANGELOG (Keep-a-Changelog), semver, ADRs for every
  public-API decision.
- SHOULD: Diátaxis tutorials/how-tos; deprecation policy once external
  consumers exist.
- SKIP: use cases, personas, UX artifacts, seeds, runbooks, ER diagrams.
- Guardrails: the public API surface is the spec — any change hits the
  CHANGELOG in the same PR; breaking change ⇒ major bump. Public type hints
  MUST be complete (types are CI-checkable — prose duplicating them is
  forbidden). Run type checker + doc generator before claiming API stability.
{% elif project_type == 'B' -%}
**Type B — CLI tool.**
- MUST: README with copy-pasteable examples, `--help` generated from command
  defs, documented exit-code table, config-file JSON Schema if a config exists.
- SHOULD: ADRs for UX decisions (flag names, defaults, breaking flag changes);
  shell-completion notes.
- SKIP: personas, UX design files, full use cases (commands are the use
  cases), seeds unless the tool owns local state.
- Guardrails: `--help` and README examples MUST agree (CI should run them).
  Renaming/removing a flag is breaking (CHANGELOG + major bump). A config
  file's schema MUST be validated at startup — unenforced schema is decoration.
{% elif project_type == 'C' -%}
**Type C — Backend API / service (no UI).**
- MUST: OpenAPI/GraphQL/Proto contract validated in CI; DB migrations (every
  schema change is a migration — no direct schema edits); seeds in three
  separated flavors (dev/test/bootstrap, A.7); C2 diagram of neighbors; ADRs;
  runbook stub once anything runs off the dev machine.
- SHOULD: event schemas in `specs/events/` if it emits/consumes; contract
  tests with known consumers; observability notes.
- SKIP: UX designs, personas, design tokens.
- Guardrails: never change an endpoint without touching the contract file in
  the same PR. Migrations are append-only in shared environments (ADR
  immutability applied to data). Grep the actual client calls before drawing
  this service in any diagram.
{% elif project_type == 'D' -%}
**Type D — Full-stack app (UI + DB).**
- MUST: everything in Type C, plus a Figma (or equiv) link in each feature
  spec; committed screenshots of shipped key flows; design tokens as code;
  use cases in `docs/use-cases/` for multi-step flows; E2E tests for them.
- SHOULD: personas (once >1 user type); component-library docs past ~10
  shared components; analytics/event-tracking spec.
- SKIP: nothing structural — Type D is the full-load case; throttle by horizon.
- Guardrails: post-launch, code + screenshots are the source of truth, not
  the Figma file — flag divergence, don't "fix" shipped UI to match a design
  file. Each use case (UC-NN) SHOULD map to ≥1 E2E test. User stories live in
  the tracker, use cases in the repo — don't copy tickets into `docs/`.
{% elif project_type == 'E' -%}
**Type E — Data pipeline / ETL / ML.**
- MUST: source schemas (mark `EXTERNAL — not under our control` where true);
  target schemas (your contract); field-level data dictionary (meaning, units,
  nullability); DAG/flow diagram; data-quality checks in CI/orchestration;
  ADRs for format/schema decisions.
- SHOULD: lineage notes; freshness/completeness SLAs once depended on; sample
  dev data.
- SKIP: UX artifacts, personas, use cases (replace with a short "business
  questions this data answers" README section).
- Guardrails: a target-schema change is a breaking API change (version +
  changelog + check downstream consumers). Every target field MUST have a data
  dictionary entry (CI-checkable). Fail loudly on source schema drift — never
  coerce silently.
{% elif project_type == 'F' -%}
**Type F — Embedded / plugin / host-bound (device, Blender, browser ext.).**
- MUST: host interface spec (which host APIs/versions — pinned); state-machine
  diagram if the component has modes; message/file-format schemas at every
  host boundary; ADRs (choices are expensive to reverse); install-into-host
  instructions in README.
- SHOULD: compatibility matrix (host versions tested); host-in-the-loop test
  plan where unit tests can't cover behavior.
- SKIP: DB migrations, seeds, runbooks (unless shipped to third parties),
  personas.
- Guardrails: host-version dependency MUST be pinned and stated near the type
  header. "Works with Blender" is UNVERIFIED; "tested on Blender 4.2,
  VERIFIED(ci log)" is a claim. Two Type-F components targeting one host in one
  repo = automatic duplication-scan trigger.
{% endif %}
## 7. Session hygiene (charter §7)
End substantive sessions by updating touched artifacts (roadmap checkbox,
changelog, spec status, new ADR). Uncommitted decisions die with the context
window. If a request conflicts with an accepted ADR, cite it and ask whether
to supersede — don't silently comply or refuse.
